# =============================================================================
# nondum — a declared function that is not implemented yet
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `@ nondum` on a top-level function declares it without a body: the
#     function is part of the interface (it can be exported and re-exported)
#     but it is unavailable, and no backend emits it
#   • without a target the function is unavailable on every target;
#     `@ nondum rs "reason"` limits the gate to one target
#   • declaring such a function costs nothing, and a program that never uses
#     it compiles and runs
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • using the function — a call, a call through an imported namespace or an
#     imported name, or taking it as a value — fails with SEM017
#     (`nondum_function_for_target`): `parse_later("a=1")` or
#     `sit _ f ← parse_later` are both refused, wherever the declaration lives
#   • leaving the body off a function that is not marked `@ nondum`
#     (PARSE012, `function_body_absent`)
#
# See also: nondum, functio, implendum, @
# =============================================================================

# meta/nondum-function — a bodiless function declared unavailable
#
# GRAMMAR:
#   nondum_functio_decl :← annotation* '@' 'nondum' payload? functio-signature
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   declared, never called
#
# BACKEND:
#   The declaration has no body and is omitted from every backend's output.

@ unstable "pending host dispatch"
fn parse_later(string text) → string

fn now(string text) → string {
    return text
}

main {
    print now("declared, never called")
}
